            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes a tensor filled with ones, in the shape you give it.\n\nMultiplying by one changes nothing, which makes ones the neutral start for many things: times a number they become a tensor full of that number, and as weights or a mask they let everything through until you change some of them.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.ones <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ... <dtype> <device>\nOne whole number per dimension: t.ones 8 is a row of 8, t.ones 4 4 a 4\nby 4 tensor. With no numbers you get a 1-element tensor.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.ones 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, to send the tensor. Nothing else makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe dimensions, typed as numbers separated by spaces or commas ('8', '4 4', '[2, 3, 4]'). A list or tensor of numbers, or a single number, sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send; the next click uses it.\n\ndtype (menu):\nThe number type. float32 is the default and what most torch operations want. The menu also offers float16, int64, uint8, bool, complex64 and complex128; 'float' is the same as float32, and each name appears a second time with a 'torch.' prefix, which means the same thing. There is no 64-bit float here.\n\ndevice (menu):\nWhere the tensor is made: cpu (the default), and mps or cuda when the machine has them. Tensors on different devices cannot be combined, so keep a chain on one device.\n\nrequires_grad (checkbox):\nRecords operations for automatic differentiation. Leave it off unless you are training something. Only float and complex tensors can carry it: ticked with an integer or bool dtype, the node raises an error and sends nothing.\n\nA dtype or device name can also be typed as an argument, after the numbers - for example t.ones 8 int64.\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor of ones:\nThe tensor. A bool dtype gives all true.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top list is the tensor itself: eight ones. Below, '*' scales it by the float. Drag the float: 't bang float' sets the multiplier first and THEN clicks t.ones, so you get a row of whatever number you drag to.\n\nRELATED:\nt.zeros is the same with every value 0. t.full makes a tensor of any one value in a single step - the same result as ones times a number. t.ones_like takes its shape from another tensor. np.ones is the NumPy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
